WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.020
Amidst fireworks, bright lights,&nbsp;
and a foreboding warning, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.100
moment in the new trailer for Season 3 of "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" has fans buzzing with excitement.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.240
There's a new trailer for the third season of&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian," and that means "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.560
fans everywhere are coming together to find every&nbsp;
small detail it teases. This includes the return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.240
of characters like Captain Carson Teva, Dr.&nbsp;
Pershing, Peli Motto, Greef Karga, and more.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.040
We also saw an allusion to the past of "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" in the form of a statue of IG-11&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.960
commemorating his sacrifice. There's also the&nbsp;
return of the iconic B1 droids, which are often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.400
the butt of jokes in series such as "Star Wars:&nbsp;
The Clone Wars" and "Star Wars: The Bad Batch."

00:00:35.521 --> 00:00:37.268
"Huh? What's happening?"

00:00:37.806 --> 00:00:39.606
"I don't know. Did we win?"

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:43.500
However, there's one detail in particular&nbsp;
that fans on Reddit found particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.520
interesting and believe might hint at what might&nbsp;
be happening in "The Mandalorian's" third season.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.320
At about one minute into the trailer, we see a&nbsp;
bustling city with tall skyscrapers and flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.220
ships everywhere. Although the galaxy likely&nbsp;
holds more than one city with that description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.640
the most iconic one in "Star Wars"&nbsp;
is almost definitely Coruscant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.460
which is a planet entirely&nbsp;
covered by a giant city.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.080
On Reddit, one user simply exclaimed,&nbsp;
"Coruscant!!!", with another adding, "Post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.940
Episode 6 Coruscant!!" This is notable because,&nbsp;
at least in the "Star Wars" shows and films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.500
we have never seen the city planet chronologically&nbsp;
after "Star Wars: Return of the Jedi." This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.100
that we would get to see Coruscant as we've&nbsp;
never seen it before, potentially gaining news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.120
on what has happened there since the dismantling&nbsp;
of the Empire and the death of Emperor Palpatine.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.560
Another Redditor wrote,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.200
This is referring to a scene in "The Force&nbsp;
Awakens" in which the First Order destroys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.920
the similar-looking city planet, which&nbsp;
led many to believe Coruscant was no more.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.300
As has been shown recently in the 2022&nbsp;
series "Andor," Coruscant is where most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.560
of the franchise's politics take place, as it is&nbsp;
the home of whatever government tends to rule the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.880
galaxy at the time, whether it be good or evil.&nbsp;
Since "The Mandalorian" takes place after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.960
Galactic Empire but before the rise of the First&nbsp;
Order, this may be one of the few times we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:05.100
to see the planet in relative peace, though&nbsp;
we know that this peace will be short-lived.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.120
Of course, fans on Reddit are excited&nbsp;
by this possibility. A user wrote,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.440
Responding to this comment, another Redditor said,

00:02:23.358 --> 00:02:27.060
This is because the New Republic decided&nbsp;
to make their home planet Hosnian Prime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.320
which doesn't seem to appear in any of the&nbsp;
trailers for "The Mandalorian" Season 3 so far.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:35.040
Although many people believe the planet is&nbsp;
Coruscant and are excited by the prospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.540
others aren't as convinced.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.720
One Reddit user believes the planet&nbsp;
might actually be Hosnian Prime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.760
which also features lots of skyscrapers and, being&nbsp;
the capital of the New Republic, might make more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.360
sense to feature if they're indeed delving&nbsp;
into that government's politics. They write,

00:02:52.468 --> 00:02:56.788
Another Redditor isn't convinced the planet&nbsp;
is either of those two, however, writing,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.240
They might be right since the planet was&nbsp;
referenced in "Andor." With this new relevance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.440
to Chandrila, it might be time to finally get a&nbsp;
closer look at the planet, especially since it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.580
served as the first capital of the New Republic&nbsp;
before it was ultimately moved to Hosnian Prime.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.840
"The Mandalorian" Season 3&nbsp;
premieres on Disney+ on March 1st.

